package com.opencode.mobile;

/** Central catalogue for model-facing and resume instructions. Keep prompts out of lifecycle/bridge code. */
final class AgentPromptCatalog {
    private AgentPromptCatalog() {}
    static final String PROGRESS_MARKER = "\n\n[OpenCode SI progress-display instruction]";
    static final String PROGRESS_INSTRUCTION = PROGRESS_MARKER + " IMPORTANT: The user needs live, visible progress in the app. Do not emit a progress message before the first tool or before any observable work. Start by taking the next concrete action. Send a short ordinary assistant text message beginning 'Progress update:' only after a real action has completed or when a long-running operation is actually about to start; state what has been checked or changed and the immediate next step. During the task, send another update after meaningful milestones. Keep updates to 1-3 concise sentences and state only observable facts: current phase, what has actually been checked/changed, next action, or a real blocker. Then continue the work without waiting for approval; do not substitute repeated plans for execution. These are user-facing status summaries, not private internal reasoning or scratchpad. Never reveal hidden chain-of-thought, private deliberation, or raw reasoning tokens. Never claim a file was changed or a command succeeded unless it actually did. If there is nothing new to report, continue working rather than emitting repetitive filler. COMMAND RELIABILITY RULES FOR TERMUX/ANDROID: Always verify `pwd` and that each target file exists before running checks; use the selected project directory, not a guessed or different workspace. Do not assume `/tmp` is writable on Android/Termux and do not use fixed paths such as `/tmp/chk.mjs` or `/tmp/err.txt`. For JavaScript syntax checks, prefer running `node --check \"$file\"` directly on each existing file; no temporary copy is needed. If a temporary file is genuinely required, first verify a writable temp directory (`TMPDIR`, `$PREFIX/tmp`, or a directory under `$HOME`) and create a unique file with `mktemp`, then clean it up with a trap. Keep stderr attached or capture it only in a confirmed-writable location. Distinguish MISSING FILE, PERMISSION/IO ERROR, SYNTAX ERROR, and PASS. Never print `FAIL` as a syntax result if Node never successfully opened/read the target file; report the actual error and stop or classify it accurately. Check each command's real exit status and do not claim success while a command is still running. If a tool call is rejected or aborted before execution, do not treat it as user cancellation or claim the command ran: report the actual tool/server error, verify task status, and retry at most once with a simpler, correctly separated command when safe. If the retry is also rejected, stop attempting that operation and report the exact blocker instead of looping through more tool calls. For ordinary shell work, use one small command per tool call; avoid long chains of unrelated checks, huge heredocs, and repeated full-project scans until a simple command succeeds. After a tool error, do not claim nothing is stuck unless a successful status check proves that; say whether the tool was rejected, whether it ran, and what the server actually returned. Prefer one short diagnostic command before repeating setup or installation.";
    static final String RESUME = "Continue the original user request from the exact point where work stopped. Do not merely repeat, rewrite, or recreate a todo list, and do not claim that todo-list creation is implementation. First inspect the current workspace and the existing conversation/tool results to determine what is actually done. Then execute the next concrete step toward the original request: read the relevant files, make the requested changes, and run appropriate checks. Preserve existing work and do not recreate the project. If a command fails, report the real error and try a reasonable alternative. Only report completion when the requested work has actually been implemented and verified.";
}
